an extra appeal to choice (since each $p_i$ is *unique*, not merely chosen, so $i\\mapsto p_i$ is a function by Replacement alone), the basic-neighbourhood convergence argument in step 2.2 correctly uses `[L4]` and directedness over a finite index set, and the final compactness conclusion is a correct application of the converse direction of `[L2]`.\n\nI independently verified the theorem's mathematical content against Wikipedia's Tychonoff and BPI pages: they state precisely that for compact Hausdorff factors, unique limits eliminate the extra choice otherwise needed to select among multiple limits, and that this is exactly why Tychonoff for compact Hausdorff spaces is provable from (and equivalent to) BPI/the ultrafilter lemma rather than full AC — the identical point the repaired step 2.1 makes. This corroborates both the theorem statement and the specific repaired reasoning against a source outside the Claude family.\n\nTitle matches what is proved; no overclaim. The four `deps` entries not tagged `[L#]` (`def-hausdorff-space`, `lem-universal-net-cluster-points-are-limits`, `thm-compactness-via-nets-filters-and-ultrafilters`, `thm-product-universal-property`) support background facts used without an explicit citation tag (Hausdorff-ness of the given factors, projection continuity) — this is citation-hygiene noise, not a false or missing citation, and falls under the library's non-strict citation convention.\n\n0 fatal, 0 nonfatal"
