research/audit/wave5-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-apply.result.json:16:  "tail": "I stopped before making any edits. Here's why.\n\n## The work is already running\n\nThe unattended driver is live and has **already dispatched this exact job**:\n\n```\n1923556  node tools/run-wave.mjs --wave 5 --judgment autonomous --from-step A3\n └─1929095  dispatch.mjs --role audit-beta --label real-analysis-apply --run wave5\n    └─1929125  claude -p --model claude-opus-5 --effort xhigh --permission-mode acceptEdits\n```\n\n`research/audit/wave5-run-state.json` is at step `A4`, and the fan-out is progressing normally — the sibling function-spaces Beta has already applied the F1 repair and bracketed it with its own snapshots:\n\n```\n11:10:08Z  pre-A4\n11:14:32Z  pre-A4-fs-F1\n11:15:18Z  post-A4-fs-F1     ← items/rem-function-space-conventions.md, thm-the-exponential-law.md written\n```\n\nIf I applied the real-analysis A4 now, PID 1929125 and I would both write the same 31 `items/*.md` files, both rewrite the three `wave5-real-analysis.*` artifacts, and both read-modify-write the shared 1 MB `wave5-touches.json`. Beyond lost writes, double-application has a specific failure here: the `L6`→`L5` renumbering in `cex-nested-open-intervals-empty` is not idempotent, and duplicate snapshots would manufacture the \"repaired more than once\" phantom that cost wave 2 an Alpha escalation to disprove.\n\n## What I did instead (read-only, useful either way)\n\nI verified the A3 approvals for my batch from disk and independently checked the parts nothing else checks:\n\n- **All 7 approved URL additions return HTTP 200**, and A3 §4d's located clause is verbatim at `sec_seqsandlims.html` line 1026: *\"A monotone sequence \\(\\{x_n\\}\\) is bounded if and only if it is convergent.\"* So the conditional `exact-source` on `cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded` stands.\n- **Both material repair targets are on disk exactly as A3 describes** — `ex-nested-intervals-single-point:74` tag `[step 1.1, L3, L4, L5, L6, L7]`, and `cex-nested-open-intervals-empty` `[L5]`/`[L6]` at lines 60/62 with `L6` used in steps 1.1 and 4.1.\n\n**Two traps the running agent may hit**, which I'd flag regardless of who applies:\n\n1. **URL over-application.** The mechanical rule \"add every ledger URL missing from the item\" yields 8 additions, but A3 approved 7. The two it must *not* add: `https://www.jirka.org/ra/` on `cor-monotone-converges-iff-bounded` (§4d specifically found that landing page carries no statement — `sec_seqsandlims.html` is the approved replacement, not a companion) and `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archimedean_property` on `rem-completeness-routes` (ledger evidence, never proposed in N6, never approved in §5b).\n2. **Snapshot ordering.** If the two material-repair items get their content fix in one pass and their provenance retag in a later bulk pass, each changes across two snapshot intervals and trips the twice-touched escalation. Folding each item's retag into the same edit as its repair avoids it.\n\n## How do you want to proceed?\n\nMy recommendation is to **let PID 1929125 finish** — it's the driver's own agent, its result lands in `wave5-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-apply.result.json`, and A6 certifies it. I can then verify its output against the independent plan above and report any divergence, which is worth doing since nothing else reads a Beta's A4 until A6.\n\nThe alternatives, if you'd rather I do it: kill the four dispatch processes (or just 1929095) and I take over the real-analysis batch, or kill the whole `run-wave.mjs` and I run A4 for all four batches. Say which and I'll proceed — I won't touch shared state until you do."
