research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/cex-paracompactness-is-not-open-hereditary--a1e876f57653fba0.result.json:16:  "tail": "```text\nVERDICT: CLEAN\n\nBOUNDARY: The counterexample uses the nonempty ordinal spaces ω₁ and ω₁+1. The complement of ω₁ in ω₁+1 is the singleton {ω₁}, which is closed (T₁), so ω₁ is open. The subspace topology on ω₁ coincides with its own order topology. No degenerate or empty cases invalidate the steps; the fixed ordinal inclusion is distinct. The statement refuted assumes AC, and every choice‑sensitive claim (compactness of ω₁+1, countable compactness of ω₁) is supported under AC via its countable choice subcase. The counterexample is a standard and robust witness.\n\nSOURCES: Inspected the full supplied text of all four cited items: `ex-omega-one-is-not-paracompact`, `prop-compact-spaces-are-paracompact`, `thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness`, `lem-ordinal-order-topology-is-t3`. Each was read and compared with its use in the target.\n\nCHECKED: Read the title, the public claim (the statement refuted), every numbered proof step (1.1, 1.2, 2.1), and the entirety of the target item. The item has no Remarks section; the logic is complete.\n```"
